Name: move folder to trash

Synonyms: move directory to trash

Type: command

Syntax: move folder <pathname> to trash

Summary:
Moves a <folder>, with everything in it, to the user's Trash or Recycle
Bin, from which it can be put back.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop

Security: disk

Example:
move folder "/Users/me/Desktop/old-project" to trash

Example:
move folder tFolderPath to trash
if the result is not empty then
   answer "Could not move the folder to the trash:" && the result
end if

Parameters:
pathname:
Specifies the name and location of the folder. If you specify a name
but not a location, the engine looks for the folder in the
<defaultFolder>.

The result:
Empty when the folder went to the trash. If there is no file or folder
at <pathname>, the result is "can't find that file". If it cannot go
to the trash for any other reason (on a phone or a server, where there
is no trash, or when the system refuses), the result is "can't move to
trash" and the folder is where it was.

Description:
Use the <move folder to trash> <command> to remove a folder the way the
desktop's own file manager does. Unlike <delete folder>, which removes
only an empty folder and for good, it moves the whole folder, with what
it holds, to the Trash (on macOS and Linux) or the Recycle Bin (on
Windows), from which the user can put it back or empty it.

It goes to the trash as <move file to trash> describes. A symbolic link
to a folder goes to the trash itself, and the folder it points to stays
where it is.

>*Note:* "trash" is no word of the language: a variable, a control or
> a custom property called trash means what it always did. This command
> is new in OXT-Beyond 0.2.8-dp.3, from HyperXTalk, where
> Emily-Elizabeth Howard wrote it.

References: move file to trash (command), delete folder (command),
revDeleteFolder (command), show in finder (command),
defaultFolder (property), folder (keyword), command (glossary)

Tags: file system
